    "slug": "florida-turf-infill-rules",
    "title": "Florida Turf Infill Rules | TIMELESS Grass & Greens",
    "description": "Florida's adopted turf rule limits residential infill to clean silica sand, rock or shell. Rubber only under playground equipment. Rule 62-308.100, 19 May 2026.",
    "h1": "Turf infill in Northeast Florida: sand, shell or rock — not rubber",
    "market": "northeast-florida",
    "updated": "2026-09-08",
    "answer": {
      "question": "What infill can be used under artificial grass in Florida?",
      "answer": "Florida Rule 62-308.100, effective 19 May 2026, sets the state minimum for synthetic turf on a single-family lot of one acre or less: infill must be clean silica sand, rock, shell or other natural material, and rubber or any other synthetic infill is allowed only within the footprint of playground equipment. Condominiums, multifamily, parks, athletic fields and commercial property sit outside the rule, and DEP does not permit, inspect or enforce it. We install artificial turf across Nassau, Duval, Clay and St. Johns counties."
    },
    "sections": [
      {
        "h2": "Why the infill choice decides drainage on a Northeast Florida lot",
        "body": [
          "Infill is the layer that decides whether a turf lawn drains or ponds. Rain travels through the infill, the backing and the base, and then it has to go into the ground — which in this part of Florida is where it stops. USDA soil survey data puts 71.4% of mapped acres in Duval, 78.5% in St. Johns and 82.2% in Nassau in the poorly or very poorly drained classes, and 75.7% of mapped St. Johns acres have the water table within six inches of the surface at some point in a normal year. NOAA's 1991-2020 normals put roughly 28.8 inches of rain on Jacksonville between June and September.",
          "Florida's rule treats those layers as one system rather than as separate products. Paragraph (2)(d) requires subgrade materials to be washed before installation. Paragraph (4) requires permeability through the turf, the backing and the subgrade, and bars compacting the soil beneath the subgrade to the point that percolation suffers. Fines migrating out of a dirty base are what turn that stack into a lid."
        ]
      },
      {
        "h2": "What Florida allows, in the rule's own words",
        "body": [
          "Rule 62-308.100, Florida Administrative Code, paragraph (2)(c): \"Infill material, if used, shall only be clean silica sand, rock, shell, or other natural material, except that coated silica sand may be used provided that any coating used is non-toxic...\" Two cross-referenced paragraphs go with it: (2)(a) prohibits heavy metals and intentionally added PFAS in the system, and (2)(b) requires it to be disposable at a landfill permitted under Chapter 62-701, F.A.C.",
          "Check the scope before assuming it covers your job. DEP's FAQ: \"This rule only applies to single-family residential properties that are 1 acre or less in size.\" Condominiums, duplexes, parks, athletic fields and commercial properties sit outside it, and so do private covenants — it \"does not address HOA requirements, deed restrictions or other private property agreements.\" Nor is the state coming to look: DEP \"does not issue permits\" for turf and \"is not implementing a permitting, inspection or enforcement program under this rule.\"",
          "So what is the rule for? It is the standard that section 125.572, Florida Statutes, hangs its protection on. Subsection (3)(a) bars a local government from prohibiting synthetic turf on single-family residential property when that turf complies with the DEP standards adopted under the section. Put rubber infill in a back yard and the installation sits outside the state standard, so that protection does not attach and your city or county stays free to regulate it. Treat the rule as the floor you have to clear, not as permission."
        ]
      },
      {
        "h2": "Rubber gets exactly one place, and it is not your lawn",
        "body": [
          "The same paragraph continues: \"Rubber or any other synthetic infill material is allowed only within the footprint of playground equipment... Installation shall be designed to prevent washing away of any infill material off the residential property.\" It is not a crumb rubber ban. \"Any other synthetic infill material\" takes TPE and EPDM beads off a residential lawn too, and leaves them available under a swing set. Coated silica sand survives, if the coating is non-toxic.",
          "There is a heat angle too. In studies collated by a 2024 systematic review, Petrass and colleagues measured SBR crumb rubber at 53.5°C against TPE at 45.6°C; Villacañas and colleagues measured SBR at 61.2°C against TPE at 58.0°C. Both differences were significant. The material Florida pushed out of residential yards is the hotter rubber, by 3.2°C to 7.9°C. A sand and organic plot in the same review measured 48.1°C — a different plot, not a head-to-head. Florida took away the hot option, not the cool one."
        ]
      },
      {
        "h2": "What actually decides how hot the surface gets",
        "body": [
