 "pet-turf": {
  "sections": [
   {
    "h2": "Choose infill within the rules for the property",
    "body": [
     "Florida answered it in a rule. Rule 62-308.100, F.A.C., effective 19 May 2026, paragraph (2)(c): \"Infill material, if used, shall only be clean silica sand, rock, shell, or other natural material,\" and rubber or any other synthetic infill is allowed \"only within the footprint of playground equipment.\" Paragraph (2)(a) separately prohibits heavy metals and intentionally added PFAS.",
     "Check the scope before assuming it covers you. DEP's FAQ says the rule \"only applies to single-family residential properties that are 1 acre or less in size,\" and that DEP \"is not implementing a permitting, inspection or enforcement program under this rule.\" A boarding kennel or a pet daycare sits outside it entirely.",
     "A 2020 laboratory study in Sports Health compared MRSA survival on several infills and measured longer survival on sand than on the other materials tested. This is a laboratory finding, not evidence of contamination on a particular dog run or a complete safety ranking. Choose the system within the applicable rules and follow its care instructions; infill does not remove the need for cleaning."
    ],
    "sources": [
     {
      "label": "Rule 62-308.100, F.A.C. — adopted rule text, Florida Administrative Code",
      "cite": "Paragraph (2)(c): \"Infill material, if used, shall only be clean silica sand, rock, shell, or other natural material, except that coated silica sand may be used provided that any coating used is non-toxic... Rubber or any other synthetic infill material is allowed only within the footprint of playground equipment.\" Paragraph (2)(a) prohibits heavy metals and intentionally added PFAS in the system. History–New 5-19-26.",
      "url": "https://www.flrules.org/gateway/readFile.asp?sid=0&tid=30839130&type=1&file=62-308.100.doc",
      "checked": "2026-09-08"
     },
     {
      "label": "Florida DEP — \"Minimum Standards for the Installation of Synthetic Turf\" Frequently Asked Questions",
      "cite": "\"This rule only applies to single-family residential properties that are 1 acre or less in size.\" \"No. DEP is not implementing a permitting, inspection or enforcement program under this rule.\"",
      "url": "https://floridadep.gov/sites/default/files/SyntheticTurfFAQ%20final.pdf",
      "checked": "2026-09-08"
     },
     {
      "label": "Sports Health (2020) — laboratory study of MRSA survival in synthetic turf systems",
      "cite": "Time to 50% loss of viability by infill: EPDM rubber 2 hours, cork-based 7, polymer-coated 9, crumb rubber 12, TPE rubber 13, sand 27 hours. MRSA recoverable up to 96 hours on infill and 24 hours on fibers (p<0.001).",
      "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7222665/",
      "checked": "2026-09-08"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "h2": "Check the whole drainage route",
    "body": [
     "Horry County's impervious-surface definition covers surfaces that prevent or impede the natural infiltration of stormwater runoff.",
     "In northeast Florida the ground is the constraint. USDA soil survey data puts 71.4% of mapped acres in Duval, 78.5% in St. Johns and 82.2% in Nassau in the poorly or very poorly drained classes, and 75.7% of mapped St. Johns acres have the water table within six inches of the surface at some point in a normal year. NOAA's 1991-2020 normals put roughly 28.8 inches of rain on Jacksonville between June and September. County-wide mapped figures do not identify the soil or drainage at an individual address. Persistent odor likewise does not establish a failed base; review care, infill and site conditions before proposing repair.",
     "Florida's rule builds against that. Paragraph (2)(d) requires subgrade materials to be washed before installation, and paragraph (4) requires permeability through the turf, the backing and the subgrade while barring compaction of the soil beneath. The closest published Front Range number belongs to a water utility rather than a building code: Thornton requires backing draining at least two inches per hour over at least three inches of compacted aggregate."
    ],
    "sources": [
     {
      "label": "New Jersey Department of Environmental Protection — Synthetic Turf: A Review of the Current Science (2025)",
      "cite": "\"Just because a turf product can drain the standard permeability rate of 10 inches per hour, for example, it does not mean the ground underneath will infiltrate at that same rate.\"",
      "url": "https://dep.nj.gov/wp-content/uploads/dsr/synthetic-turf-report-2025.pdf",
      "checked": "2026-09-08"
     },
     {
      "label": "Horry County Code of Ordinances, Sec. 17.7-16 (stormwater utility definitions)",
      "cite": "\"Common impervious surfaces include, but are not limited to, rooftops, sidewalks, walkways, patio areas, driveways, parking lots, storage areas, compacted gravel and soil surfaces, awnings and other fabric or plastic coverings...\" The definition closes on surfaces which prevent or impede the natural infiltration of stormwater runoff. Artificial turf is not named. Code current through 12 August 2026.",
      "url": "https://library.municode.com/sc/horry_county/codes/code_of_ordinances?nodeId=COOR_CH17.7STMA_ARTIISTUTFE_S17.7-16DE",
      "checked": "2026-09-08"
     },
     {
      "label": "NOAA NCEI 1991-2020 climate normals, Jacksonville International Airport (USW00013889)",
      "cite": "Normal monthly precipitation June 7.60 in, July 6.77 in, August 6.88 in, September 7.56 in — roughly 28.8 inches across the four months.",
      "url": "https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/access/services/data/v1?dataset=normals-monthly-1991-2020&stations=USW00013889&dataTypes=MLY-TMAX-NORMAL,MLY-TMIN-NORMAL,MLY-PRCP-NORMAL&format=json",
      "checked": "2026-09-08"
     },
     {
      "label": "Rule 62-308.100, F.A.C. — adopted rule text, Florida Administrative Code",
      "cite": "(2)(d) requires washed subgrade materials; (4) requires permeability through the turf, backing and subgrade and bars compacting the soil beneath the subgrade to the point that percolation suffers.",
      "url": "https://www.flrules.org/gateway/readFile.asp?sid=0&tid=30839130&type=1&file=62-308.100.doc",
      "checked": "2026-09-08"
     },
     {
      "label": "Thornton Water — 2026 Artificial Turf Rules for the Water-Wise Landscape Rebate",
      "cite": "Requires backing that drains at a minimum of two inches per hour, a sub-base of at least three inches of compacted aggregate, glued rather than sewn seams, a minor development permit before installation, an eight-year fade warranty, and PFAS-free documentation.",
      "url": "https://www.thorntonwater.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/2026-residential_artificial_turf_info__rules-1.pdf",
      "checked": "2026-09-08"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "h2": "Plan shade and check surface conditions",
    "body": [
     "A 2024 systematic review of 23 studies recorded 30 mm synthetic fibers averaging 57.5°C in sun against 32.2°C in shade, with maxima of 80.1°C and 42.1°C. Sun and shade move the surface by 25°C in that data. Where a dog run goes is worth deciding before what goes in it.",
     "The same review found air over synthetic only 0.5 to 1.2°C warmer than over natural grass, with no significant difference in wet bulb globe temperature. Those findings highlight surface contact for paws, bare feet and a dog lying down. They do not establish that every shaded surface is comfortable or that other heat conditions can be ignored.",
     "Hosing buys minutes, and the minutes are measured. In Brigham Young University readings a synthetic surface fell from 174°F to 85°F under irrigation, then read 120°F after five minutes and 164°F after twenty. Penn State found that mixing 20% calcined clay into infill to hold moisture \"did not reduce surface temperature.\""
    ],
    "sources": [
     {
      "label": "Williams & Pulley, Brigham Young University — full-day infrared surface temperature measurements on adjacent synthetic and natural fields",
      "cite": "Irrigation dropped the synthetic surface from 174°F to 85°F; after five minutes it was 120°F and after twenty minutes 164°F.",
      "url": "https://www.westcoastturf.com/getdoc.cfm?id=38",
      "checked": "2026-09-08"
     }
    ]
   }
  ],
