 "commercial-turf": {
  "sections": [
   {
    "h2": "Which commercial properties the Colorado rule actually reaches",
    "body": [
     "Applicable property is defined at C.R.S. 37-99-102(1) and covers commercial, institutional and industrial property together with common interest community property, which 37-99-102(5) describes as the land a unit owners' association owns and maintains, entryways, parks and other common elements. HB25-1113 added one more category: applicable residential real property at 37-99-102(1.5), meaning multifamily housing with more than twelve dwelling units.",
     "Two limits sit in the same statute. Anything installed before 1 January 2026 is preserved by 37-99-103(4)(a), and a redevelopment project only counts once it disturbs more than fifty percent of the aggregate landscape area under 37-99-102(14).",
     "The date for the multifamily category is contested. Colorado's Division of Real Estate told its 2025 HOA Forum that the twelve-plus-unit expansion applies on or after 1 January 2026. Western Resource Advocates' compliance guide and the Town of Fraser's staff report both read it as a 1 January 2028 obligation. We have not resolved which reading is right. C.R.S. 37-99-103(4)(d) also lets a local entity be more stringent than the state floor, and 37-99-102(9) puts special and metropolitan districts inside the definition of local entity. The section numbers, the dates and the definitions are set out in full in <a href=\"/guides/colorado-turf-law/\">Colorado's artificial turf law</a>."
    ],
    "sources": [
     {
      "label": "Colorado SB24-005, signed act (2024)",
      "cite": "37-99-102(1) applicable property — commercial, institutional and industrial property and common interest community property; 37-99-102(5) common interest community property as the land a unit owners' association owns and maintains, entryways, parks and other common elements; 37-99-102(14) redevelopment project and the fifty percent aggregate landscape area trigger; 37-99-103(4)(a) grandfathering of pre-2026 installations; 37-99-103(4)(d) more-stringent local authority; 37-99-102(9) local entity includes special and metropolitan districts.",
      "url": "https://content.leg.colorado.gov/sites/default/files/2024a_005_signed.pdf",
      "checked": "2026-09-08"
     },
     {
      "label": "Colorado Division of Real Estate — 2025 HOA Forum legislative summaries",
      "cite": "States the twelve-plus-unit expansion applies \"on or after January 1, 2026\".",
      "url": "https://dre.colorado.gov/sites/dre/files/documents/2025-07-25%20HOA%20Forum%202025%20Legislative%20Summaries.pdf",
      "checked": "2026-09-08"
     },
     {
      "label": "Western Resource Advocates — SB24-005 simplified compliance guide",
      "cite": "Reads the multifamily obligation as 1 January 2028; states that only the disturbed portion of landscaping on redeveloped parcels must conform.",
      "url": "https://westernresourceadvocates.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/2025_SB24-005_Simplified-Guide.pdf",
      "checked": "2026-09-08"
     },
     {
      "label": "Town of Fraser — staff report on local compliance with SB24-005 and HB25-1113",
      "cite": "Municipal implementation reading the multifamily obligation as 1 January 2028; an example of a local entity adopting a conforming code text amendment.",
      "url": "https://www.frasercolorado.com/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Item/7839?fileID=10590",
      "checked": "2026-09-08"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "h2": "Check the commercial project’s actual requirements",
    "body": [
     "Florida wrote a synthetic turf standard and then aimed it at houses. Rule 62-308.100, F.A.C., took effect 19 May 2026, and DEP's own FAQ states that it \"only applies to single-family residential properties that are 1 acre or less in size.\" Condominiums, multifamily, parks, athletic fields and commercial property sit outside it. What the rule requires on the residential side is set out in <a href=\"/guides/florida-turf-infill-rules/\">Florida's turf infill rule</a>.",
     "That matters because the rule is what triggers the protection. Section 125.572(3)(a), Florida Statutes, bars a local government from prohibiting compliant synthetic turf on single-family residential property. A commercial parcel gets no such shield. In Atlantic Beach, development resulting in more than 35 percent impervious lot coverage must provide on-site stormwater storage for the entire impervious surface area.",
     "For a South Carolina commercial project, ask the responsible reviewer how the proposed surface is classified and what documentation is required. Do not treat a search that found no turf-specific provision as permission to proceed. Horry County Sec. 17.7-16 lists compacted gravel surfaces and plastic coverings among common impervious surfaces, and Sec. 17.7-17 assigns non-single-family parcels a development intensity factor by impervious percentage, 0.95 at 90 to 100 percent down to 0.01 below one percent, billed per 20,000 square feet of land area. We set out the rest of it in <a href=\"/guides/is-artificial-turf-impervious/\">stormwater and built-upon area, county by county</a>."
    ],
    "sources": [
     {
      "label": "Florida Administrative Code rule record — 62-308.100 \"Synthetic Turf\"",
      "cite": "Chapter 62-308, \"Minimum Standards for the Installation of Synthetic Turf on Specified Properties\". Effective Date: 5/19/2026. History note \"History–New 5-19-26\". Rulemaking Authority and Law Implemented: 125.572 FS.",
      "url": "https://www.flrules.org/gateway/ruleNo.asp?id=62-308.100",
      "checked": "2026-09-08"
     },
     {
      "label": "Florida DEP — \"Minimum Standards for the Installation of Synthetic Turf\" Frequently Asked Questions",
      "cite": "\"This rule only applies to single-family residential properties that are 1 acre or less in size.\" \"DEP does not issue permits or require authorization for the installation of synthetic turf.\" \"No. DEP is not implementing a permitting, inspection or enforcement program under this rule.\"",
      "url": "https://floridadep.gov/sites/default/files/SyntheticTurfFAQ%20final.pdf",
      "checked": "2026-09-08"
     },
     {
      "label": "Section 125.572, Florida Statutes — Regulation of synthetic turf",
      "cite": "Created by HB 683 (2025), ch. 2025-140. Subsection (3)(a) bars a local government from adopting or enforcing any ordinance, resolution, order, rule or policy that prohibits a property owner from installing synthetic turf \"that complies with Department of Environmental Protection standards adopted pursuant to this section\" on single-family residential property, once DEP adopts rules under subsection (4).",
      "url": "https://www.leg.state.fl.us/Statutes/index.cfm?App_mode=Display_Statute&Search_String=&URL=0100-0199%2F0125%2FSections%2F0125.572.html",
      "checked": "2026-09-08"
     },
     {
      "label": "Atlantic Beach Code Sec. 24-17 and Sec. 24-89(c)(1)",
      "cite": "Impervious surface means surfaces \"made of concrete, asphalt, brick, plastic, or any solid surfacing material with a base or lining of an impervious material\". Development resulting in more than 35 percent impervious lot coverage must provide on-site stormwater storage for the entire impervious surface area, with a cumulative 400 square foot exemption.",
      "url": "https://library.municode.com/fl/atlantic_beach/codes/code_of_ordinances?nodeId=PTIICOOR_CH24LADERE_ARTIILADE_S24-17DE",
      "checked": "2026-09-08"
     },
     {
      "label": "Horry County Code Sec. 17.7-16 (Stormwater Utility, Definitions)",
      "cite": "\"Common impervious surfaces include, but are not limited to, rooftops, sidewalks, walkways, patio areas, driveways, parking lots, storage areas, compacted gravel and soil surfaces, awnings and other fabric or plastic coverings, and other surfaces which prevent or impede the natural infiltration of stormwater runoff which existed prior to development.\" Artificial turf is not named. Full-text searches of the Horry County, Myrtle Beach and North Myrtle Beach codes return no occurrence of artificial turf, synthetic turf, artificial grass or synthetic grass.",
      "url": "https://library.municode.com/sc/horry_county/codes/code_of_ordinances?nodeId=COOR_CH17.7STMA_ARTIISTUTFE_S17.7-16DE",
      "checked": "2026-09-08"
     },
     {
      "label": "Horry County Code Sec. 17.7-17 (Stormwater service charge)",
      "cite": "Non-single-family parcels are assigned a development intensity factor by impervious percentage — 90–100% = 0.95 down to less than 1% = 0.01 — billed per 20,000 square feet of land area.",
      "url": "https://library.municode.com/sc/horry_county/codes/code_of_ordinances?nodeId=COOR_CH17.7STMA_ARTIISTUTFE_S17.7-17STSECHRA",
      "checked": "2026-09-08"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "h2": "Commercial rebate eligibility is provider-specific",
    "body": [
     "Denver Water runs a Landscape Transformation Assistance Program for HOA and commercial projects that funds up to half of project cost, and its published list of what it will not fund names \"designs that include artificial turf, landscape fabrics/weed barriers or hardscapes.\" Castle Rock Water states, for both its residential and non-residential programs, that \"Artificial turf does not qualify for any part of the rebate.\"",
     "The one metro provider we found that pays for artificial turf is Thornton, whose 2026 artificial turf rules are scoped to single-family detached and attached dwellings. We found no Denver-metro commercial or HOA rebate for it, and the state grant would not have covered it either: C.R.S. 37-60-135 bars applicants from using Turf Replacement Program money to replace turf with artificial turf, and the Colorado Water Conservation Board stopped accepting applications in March 2025. Provider by provider, that is <a href=\"/guides/colorado-water-rebates-and-turf/\">which Denver-metro water providers actually pay</a>."
    ],
    "sources": [
     {
      "label": "Thornton Water — 2026 Artificial Turf Rules for Water-Wise Landscape Rebate (PDF)",
      "cite": "Up to $2 per square foot on qualifying 200–1,000 square foot projects, lifetime maximum $2,000 per residential lot; scope limited to the front, back and side yards of single-family detached and attached dwellings; Minor Development Permit required before installation; backing must drain a minimum of two inches per hour; PFAS-free documentation required.",
      "url": "https://www.thorntonwater.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/2026-residential_artificial_turf_info__rules-1.pdf",
      "checked": "2026-09-08"
     },
     {
      "label": "Colorado Water Conservation Board — Turf Replacement Program",
      "cite": "\"Update: As of March 2025, CWCB is no longer accepting applications for the Turf Replacement Grant Program.\" Statute (HB22-1151, C.R.S. 37-60-135): applicants \"SHALL NOT USE THE MONEY TO REPLACE TURF WITH ANY OF THE FOLLOWING: (A) IMPERMEABLE CONCRETE; (B) ARTIFICIAL TURF; (C) WATER FEATURES SUCH AS FOUNTAINS; (D) INVASIVE PLANT SPECIES; OR (E) TURF.\" CWCB lists \"Single Family Homeowners\" and \"Homeowners' Associations or any Multifamily Property\" as INELIGIBLE ENTITIES.",
      "url": "https://cwcb.colorado.gov/turf-replacement-program",
      "checked": "2026-09-08"
     }
    ]
   }
