   {
    "h2": "Plan the field’s stormwater review",
    "body": [
     "Plan the stormwater review alongside a field’s excavation, base and drainage design. In unincorporated Horry County, ask the county to determine the requirements for the full scope, including its relationship to a larger development. Do not use field-versus-backyard size as a substitute for that determination.",
     "Whether the finished surface counts against impervious coverage then differs by state. North Carolina answers by statute: G.S. 143-214.7D(b)(6) provides that artificial turf manufactured to drain through its backing and installed to manufacturer specification over a pervious surface is not built-upon area, and subsection (d) preempts a conflicting local definition. For South Carolina, confirm the proposed field’s treatment with the responsible reviewer; a general definition or a search result does not decide the project. Horry County Sec. 17.7-16 never names turf, though it lists compacted gravel and soil surfaces and other plastic coverings among common impervious surfaces.",
     "Florida wrote a synthetic turf rule and left fields outside it. DEP's FAQ on Rule 62-308.100 asks whether the rule applies to condominiums, duplexes, parks, athletic fields or commercial properties and answers no; it reaches single-family lots of one acre or less. A field in Duval or Nassau County gets neither the state minimum standard nor the preemption that comes with it. Colorado regulates turf for water use rather than runoff, and we found no Front Range ordinance classifying it for coverage."
    ],
    "sources": [
     {
      "label": "Horry County: current stormwater permitting guidance",
      "cite": "The page gives inconsistent land-disturbance thresholds and describes staff review of exemption claims. Ask the county to determine the applicable requirements for the full project scope.",
      "url": "https://www.horrycountysc.gov/departments/stormwater/engineers/construction/storm-water-permitting/",
      "checked": "2026-09-11"
     },
     {
      "label": "Horry County Stormwater Management Design Manual (January 2024)",
      "cite": "Historical manual source. County staff should confirm current requirements and any exemption for the actual project; the live permitting page contains inconsistent thresholds.",
      "url": "https://www.horrycountysc.gov/media/4wqkxbxg/horry-county-sw-design-manual_rev2024.pdf",
      "checked": "2026-09-08"
     },
     {
      "label": "N.C. Gen. Stat. 143-214.7D",
      "cite": "Subsection (b)(6) excludes from built-upon area \"artificial turf, manufactured to allow water to drain through the backing of the turf, and installed according to the manufacturer's specifications over a pervious surface\"; (d) preempts a local definition of built-upon area or impervious surface that does not comply with subsection (b). Enacted by S.L. 2024-49 s. 4.48(b).",
      "url": "https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/HTML/BySection/Chapter_143/GS_143-214.7D.html",
