 "indoor-turf": {
  "sections": [
   {
    "h2": "Colorado's 2026 restriction is a landscape rule",
    "body": [
     "Senate Bill 24-005 created Article 99 in Title 37 of the Colorado Revised Statutes. C.R.S. 37-99-103(1) tells local entities that from 1 January 2026 they must not install, or allow anyone to install, nonfunctional turf, artificial turf or invasive plant species as part of a new development or redevelopment project on commercial, institutional, industrial and HOA-common property. House Bill 25-1113 inserted the word NONFUNCTIONAL in front of artificial turf and added a definition of functional artificial turf at C.R.S. 37-99-102(6.5) covering playgrounds, sports fields and the playing area of a golf course. SB24-005 had already excepted athletic fields of play.",
     "Two things in that text point away from an indoor training floor. A sports field is named as functional. And a redevelopment project only counts under 37-99-102(14) once it disturbs more than fifty percent of the aggregate landscape area, which is a measurement of landscaping. Article 99 governs water use; it does not classify turf for stormwater or built-upon-area purposes.",
     "That is a reading, not a settled question. We hold no Colorado source classifying an interior floor either way, and 37-99-103(4)(d) lets a local entity be more stringent than the state floor — 37-99-102(9) counts metropolitan districts as local entities. Ask for the adopted text before material is ordered."
    ],
    "sources": [
     {
      "label": "Colorado SB24-005, signed act (2024)",
      "cite": "Creates Title 37 Article 99. 37-99-103(1) prohibition on \"nonfunctional turf, artificial turf, or invasive plant species\" from 1 January 2026; 37-99-102(1) applicable property (commercial, institutional and industrial property) and 37-99-102(5) common interest community property; 37-99-102(14) redevelopment project and the fifty percent aggregate landscape area trigger; 37-99-103(4)(d) more-stringent local authority; 37-99-102(9) local entity includes special and metropolitan districts; athletic fields of play exception.",
      "url": "https://content.leg.colorado.gov/sites/default/files/2024a_005_signed.pdf",
      "checked": "2026-09-08"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "h2": "Choose the indoor system for the facility",
    "body": [
     "Rule 62-308.100, Florida Administrative Code, took effect 19 May 2026 and limits infill on a residential lot to clean silica sand, rock, shell or other natural material, with rubber allowed only within the footprint of playground equipment. It does not reach a gym. DEP's own FAQ answers the scope question in a line: \"Does this rule apply to condominiums, duplexes, parks, athletic fields or commercial properties? No. This rule only applies to single-family residential properties that are 1 acre or less in size.\"",
     "That cuts both ways. The infill restriction does not bind an indoor facility, and neither does the protection it unlocks — section 125.572(3)(a), Florida Statutes, bars a local government from prohibiting compliant synthetic turf on single-family residential property, and a training center is not that.",
     "Indoor turf, any pad, attachment and infill should be selected together for the floor and intended activities. A laboratory study of bacteria on infill does not establish a cleaning schedule for a particular gym. Follow the selected product’s care instructions, and confirm reopening time before scheduling use. The Jaguars’ Miller Electric Center is an outside example using coconut fiber and cork infill, not a Timeless project or specification for every indoor floor."
    ],
    "sources": [
     {
      "label": "Rule 62-308.100, F.A.C. — adopted rule text",
      "cite": "\"Infill material, if used, shall only be clean silica sand, rock, shell, or other natural material, except that coated silica sand may be used provided that any coating used is non-toxic... Rubber or any other synthetic infill material is allowed only within the footprint of playground equipment.\" History–New 5-19-26.",
      "url": "https://www.flrules.org/gateway/readFile.asp?sid=0&tid=30839130&type=1&file=62-308.100.doc",
      "checked": "2026-09-08"
     },
     {
      "label": "Florida Administrative Code rule record — 62-308.100 \"Synthetic Turf\"",
      "cite": "Chapter 62-308, \"Minimum Standards for the Installation of Synthetic Turf on Specified Properties\". Effective Date: 5/19/2026. Rulemaking Authority and Law Implemented: 125.572 FS.",
      "url": "https://www.flrules.org/gateway/ruleNo.asp?id=62-308.100",
      "checked": "2026-09-08"
     },
     {
      "label": "Florida DEP — \"Minimum Standards for the Installation of Synthetic Turf\" Frequently Asked Questions",
      "cite": "\"Does this rule apply to condominiums, duplexes, parks, athletic fields or commercial properties? No. This rule only applies to single-family residential properties that are 1 acre or less in size.\"",
      "url": "https://floridadep.gov/sites/default/files/SyntheticTurfFAQ%20final.pdf",
      "checked": "2026-09-08"
     },
     {
      "label": "Section 125.572, Florida Statutes — Regulation of synthetic turf",
      "cite": "Created by HB 683 (2025), ch. 2025-140. Subsection (3)(a) bars a local government from prohibiting a property owner from installing synthetic turf \"that complies with Department of Environmental Protection standards adopted pursuant to this section\" on single-family residential property.",
      "url": "https://www.leg.state.fl.us/Statutes/index.cfm?App_mode=Display_Statute&Search_String=&URL=0100-0199%2F0125%2FSections%2F0125.572.html",
      "checked": "2026-09-08"
     },
     {
      "label": "Sports Health (2020) — laboratory study of MRSA survival in synthetic turf systems",
      "cite": "MRSA recoverable up to 96 hours on infill and 24 hours on fibers (p<0.001). Time to 50% loss of viability by infill: EPDM rubber 2 hours, cork-based 7, polymer-coated 9, crumb rubber 12, TPE rubber 13, sand 27 hours.",
      "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7222665/",
      "checked": "2026-09-08"
     }
    ]
   }
  ],
