 "turf-removal-and-replacement": {
  "sections": [
   {
    "h2": "North Carolina wrote the base standard down",
    "body": [
     "Two states have written down how artificial turf should be installed. Florida did it in 2026 as an environmental rule. North Carolina did it in 2016 as a licensing rule, and North Carolina's is the older and more specific of the two. 21 NCAC 28B .0503(b), effective 1 September 2016 and amended 1 July 2021, requires a licensed landscape contractor to compact and pitch the sub-grade to drain, establish a perimeter attachment system, roll the turf with the nap facing a consistent direction, secure it with an evenly weighted sand layer, and follow the manufacturer's specifications.",
     "The same standard carries money on the Carolina coast. G.S. 143-214.7D(b)(6) excludes artificial turf from built-upon area only where it is manufactured to drain through the backing and installed to the manufacturer's specifications over a pervious surface, and (b)(3) separately excludes number 57 stone at least four inches thick over geotextile fabric. The stone provision and artificial-turf provision are separate exclusions. Assess the proposed turf system against its applicable conditions rather than assuming that a particular stone depth decides the turf classification.",
     "North Myrtle Beach put maintenance into the same test. Sec. 23-129.4, added by Ordinance 25-45 on 6 October 2025, requires a surface to demonstrate a minimum infiltration rate of two inches per hour, be installed to manufacturer specification, pass inspection and be maintained, and states that if these criteria are not met the surface will be considered impervious, regardless of material type or labeling."
    ],
    "sources": [
     {
      "label": "21 NCAC 28B .0503(b) — North Carolina Landscape Contractors' Licensing Board rules",
      "cite": "Artificial turf workmanship standard: \"Ensure that the sub-grade is compacted and shall pitch properly to drain\", plus a perimeter attachment system, nap facing a consistent direction, an evenly weighted sand layer over the whole surface, and the manufacturer's specifications. Effective 1 September 2016, amended 1 July 2021.",
      "url": "http://reports.oah.state.nc.us/ncac/title%2021%20-%20occupational%20licensing%20boards%20and%20commissions/chapter%2028%20-%20landscape%20contractors/chapter%2028%20rules.pdf",
      "checked": "2026-09-08"
     },
     {
      "label": "N.C. Gen. Stat. 143-214.7D — Stormwater runoff rules and programs",
      "cite": "Subsection (b)(6) excludes from built-upon area \"Artificial turf, manufactured to allow water to drain through the backing of the turf, and installed according to the manufacturer's specifications over a pervious surface\"; (b)(3) excludes #57 stone at least four inches thick over geotextile fabric; (d) bars a local government from enforcing a definition of built-upon area that does not comply with subsection (b). Enacted by S.L. 2024-49 s. 4.48(b).",
      "url": "https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/HTML/BySection/Chapter_143/GS_143-214.7D.html",
      "checked": "2026-09-08"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "h2": "Check the requirements for the replacement",
    "body": [
     "Rule 62-308.100, Florida Administrative Code, took effect on 19 May 2026. Check its scope and requirements for a proposed replacement on a single-family lot of one acre or less. The existing installation’s date and materials need confirmation; do not assume every lawn predates the rule.",
     "The rule reaches the base and not only the carpet. Paragraph (2)(d) requires subgrade materials to be washed before installation. Paragraph (4) requires permeability through the turf, the backing and the subgrade, and bars compacting the soil beneath the subgrade to the point that percolation suffers. Paragraph (9)(b) requires anchoring at all edges and seams.",
     "Two more paragraphs decide what can go back down. Paragraph (2)(a) prohibits heavy metals and intentionally added PFAS in the system, and (2)(b) requires it to be disposable under normal conditions at any landfill permitted under Chapter 62-701, F.A.C. Florida's own standard for old turf is landfill disposability, not recyclability."
    ],
    "sources": [
     {
      "label": "Rule 62-308.100, F.A.C. — adopted rule text, Florida Administrative Code",
      "cite": "(2)(a) heavy metals and intentionally added PFAS prohibited; (2)(b) the system must be disposable under normal conditions at any Chapter 62-701, F.A.C. permitted landfill; (2)(c) infill limited to clean silica sand, rock, shell or other natural material; (2)(d) washed subgrade; (4) permeability through turf, backing and subgrade and no compaction of the soil beneath the subgrade to the extent it adversely impacts percolation; (9)(b) anchoring at all edges and seams. History–New 5-19-26.",
      "url": "https://www.flrules.org/gateway/readFile.asp?sid=0&tid=30839130&type=1&file=62-308.100.doc",
      "checked": "2026-09-08"
     },
     {
      "label": "Florida Administrative Code rule record — 62-308.100 \"Synthetic Turf\"",
      "cite": "Chapter 62-308, \"Minimum Standards for the Installation of Synthetic Turf on Specified Properties\". Effective Date: 5/19/2026. History note \"History–New 5-19-26\". Rulemaking Authority and Law Implemented: 125.572 FS.",
      "url": "https://www.flrules.org/gateway/ruleNo.asp?id=62-308.100",
      "checked": "2026-09-08"
     },
     {
      "label": "Florida DEP — \"Minimum Standards for the Installation of Synthetic Turf\" Frequently Asked Questions",
      "cite": "\"This rule only applies to single-family residential properties that are 1 acre or less in size.\" \"DEP does not issue permits or require authorization for the installation of synthetic turf.\" \"No. DEP is not implementing a permitting, inspection or enforcement program under this rule.\"",
      "url": "https://floridadep.gov/sites/default/files/SyntheticTurfFAQ%20final.pdf",
      "checked": "2026-09-08"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "h2": "In Colorado the replacement is a different event from the original",
    "body": [
     "Colorado preserves what is already down and asks a fresh question about what replaces it. C.R.S. 37-99-103(4)(a) grandfathers anything installed before 1 January 2026. The prohibition at 37-99-103(1), narrowed by HB25-1113 to nonfunctional artificial turf, applies to new development and redevelopment projects on commercial, institutional and industrial parcels under 37-99-102(1), on the common elements an association owns and maintains under 37-99-102(5), and on multifamily housing with more than twelve dwelling units under 37-99-102(1.5).",
     "A redevelopment project is defined at 37-99-102(14) by disturbing more than fifty percent of the aggregate landscape area. On an HOA common area or a commercial parcel, that threshold is what decides whether failed nonfunctional turf can be rebuilt as it was. On a single-family lot Article 99 does not apply in the first place.",
     "Thornton is the only Denver-metro water provider we found that pays for artificial turf, and its 2026 rules require the turf to replace an area of high-water demand lawn, such as Kentucky Bluegrass, or areas of no landscaping on new construction. Turf replacing turf is neither."
    ],
    "sources": [
     {
      "label": "Colorado SB24-005, signed act (2024)",
      "cite": "Creates Title 37 Article 99. 37-99-103(1) prohibition applying to new development and redevelopment projects on applicable property; 37-99-102(1) applicable property (commercial, institutional, industrial and common interest community property); 37-99-102(5) common interest community property as the land a unit owners' association owns and maintains; 37-99-102(14) redevelopment project and the fifty percent aggregate landscape area trigger; 37-99-103(4)(a) grandfathering of installations before 1 January 2026; residential property expressly excluded.",
      "url": "https://content.leg.colorado.gov/sites/default/files/2024a_005_signed.pdf",
      "checked": "2026-09-08"
     },
     {
      "label": "Thornton Water — 2026 Artificial Turf Rules for the Water-Wise Landscape Rebate",
      "cite": "\"To be eligible for the rebate, artificial turf must replace an area of high-water demand lawn, such as Kentucky Bluegrass, on existing residential lots or areas of no landscaping on new construction residential lots.\" Also requires a minor development permit before installation, backing draining a minimum of two inches per hour, at least three inches of compacted aggregate sub-base, an eight-year fade warranty, and third-party or manufacturer PFAS-free documentation.",
      "url": "https://www.thorntonwater.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/2026-residential_artificial_turf_info__rules-1.pdf",
      "checked": "2026-09-08"
     }
    ]
   }
