 "grand-strand": {
  "sections": [
   {
    "h2": "Use soil mapping to frame site questions",
    "body": [
     "Horry County's USDA soil survey divides 736,000 acres into twelve general soil map units. The largest, Yauhannah-Ogeechee-Bladen, is 19 percent of the county and rated moderately well to poorly drained. Woodington-Goldsboro-Pocomoke is another 15.5 percent, rated poorly, moderately well and very poorly drained. The one unit with excessively drained soils, Lakeland-Leon-Newhan on the dunes and ridges along the ocean, is 4.5 percent of the county — 32 percent Lakeland against 15 percent poorly drained Leon.",
     "Those names carry published water table depths. Under Leon fine sand the water table sits 6 to 18 inches down for one to four months in most years. Woodington runs 0 to 12 inches, December to May. Ogeechee ranges from a foot down to a foot of standing water above the surface. Across the state line, NRCS rates Leon fine sand \"Very limited\" for small commercial buildings in Brunswick County, and gives the reason as depth to saturated zone at 1.00 — the top of the severity scale.",
     "The sand itself moves water quickly: Baymeade is rated 6 to 20 inches per hour through its top three feet. On the Leon, Woodington and Ogeechee flats, the water table sits between the surface and 18 inches down for months at a time. These regional descriptions cannot establish the soil, water table or required base at a particular property. The free visit starts with the actual work area and visible drainage questions."
    ],
    "sources": [
     {
      "label": "USDA Official Series Description — LEON",
      "cite": "\"The water table is at depths of 6 to 18 inches for 1 to 4 months during most years.\"",
      "url": "https://soilseries.sc.egov.usda.gov/OSD_Docs/L/LEON.html",
      "checked": "2026-09-08"
     },
     {
      "label": "NRCS Web Soil Survey ratings for Brunswick County NC, Small Commercial Buildings",
      "cite": "Leon fine sand rated \"Very limited\", rating reason \"Depth to saturated zone (1.00)\" — 1.00 being the maximum severity value on the NRCS 0.00-1.00 scale. Baymeade fine sand, 1 to 6 percent slopes, is rated \"Not limited\".",
      "url": "https://brunswickcountync.gov/DocumentCenter/View/6120/Soil-Survey-Small-Commercial-Buildings",
      "checked": "2026-09-08"
     },
     {
      "label": "USDA Official Series Description — BAYMEADE",
      "cite": "Saturated hydraulic conductivity 6.0 to 20 in/hr from 0 to 36 inches.",
      "url": "https://soilseries.sc.egov.usda.gov/OSD_Docs/B/BAYMEADE.html",
      "checked": "2026-09-08"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "h2": "County review and association permission are separate",
    "body": [
     "Confirm the actual property jurisdiction. For a Carolina Forest proposal in unincorporated Horry County, ask the county about excavation, grading, drainage and related construction before work starts. Obtain any required association response separately.",
     "Association documents are a separate part of the property review. Ask for the current covenants and architectural requirements that apply to the proposal. S.C. Code 27-30-130 makes governing documents enforceable only once recorded with the county Register of Deeds, and requires rules and amendments to be re-recorded by January 10 each year to stay enforceable.",
     "South Carolina has 7,600 community associations holding 593,000 housing units. On the North Carolina side the association is built into the subdivision by ordinance: Brunswick County requires that roads not maintained by NCDOT be dedicated to a property owners association, that the association be established before lots are sold, and that membership be mandatory for every buyer."
    ],
    "sources": [
     {
      "label": "South Carolina Homeowners Association Act, S.C. Code Title 27, Chapter 30",
      "cite": "Sec. 27-30-130 requires governing documents to be recorded in the county Register of Deeds/RMC to be enforceable, and requires rules, regulations and amendments to be re-recorded by January tenth of each year following their adoption or amendment to remain enforceable. The chapter places no substantive limit on landscaping or architectural restrictions and contains no mention of artificial turf.",
      "url": "https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t27c030.php",
      "checked": "2026-09-08"
     },
     {
      "label": "Foundation for Community Association Research, 2025 Statistical Review — Community Associations by State",
