 "denver-metro": {
  "sections": [
   {
    "h2": "Review the ground at the project address",
    "body": [
     "The Colorado Geological Survey calls swelling soil and rock one of the most significant, widespread and costly, but least publicized, geologic hazards in Colorado. About half of the roughly thirty sedimentary bedrock formations exposed in the Front Range Urban Corridor contain swelling clay, and CGS says they underlie all of the major cities.",
     "CGS puts swell pressures at 20,000 pounds per square foot or greater, with soils expanding up to twenty percent by volume when they get wet. In a study of 9,040 Denver-area houses that CGS reports, the 1,056 built on near-surface Pierre Shale had a 38.44 percent damage rate against an 8.95 percent average.",
     "Infiltration is slow on top of that. Across the six USDA-NRCS survey areas covering this service footprint, 64.3 percent of mapped component acreage sits in hydrologic soil group C or D, the slow and very slow classes. CGS describes granular fill distributing heave in an outwardly shifting, grain-by-grain manner, discourages plastic sheeting because it traps moisture, and recommends a ten percent slope within the first ten feet of a foundation. These regional studies and foundation recommendations are not a turf-installation specification or a guarantee against movement. Preparation needs to fit the actual work area."
    ],
    "sources": [
     {
      "label": "Colorado Geological Survey — Potentially Swelling Soil and Rock in the Front Range Urban Corridor, Colorado",
      "cite": "CGS calls swelling clay \"one of the most significant, widespread, and costly, but least publicized, geologic hazards in Colorado.\" Half of the roughly 30 sedimentary bedrock formations exposed in the Front Range Urban Corridor contain swelling clay, and these swelling formations \"underlie all of the major cities.\" Checked 2026-09-07.",
      "url": "https://coloradogeologicalsurvey.org/publications/potentially-swelling-soil-rock-front-range-urban-corridor-colorado/",
      "checked": "2026-09-08"
     },
     {
      "label": "Colorado Geological Survey — Expansive Soil and Rock hazard page",
      "cite": "Swell pressures of \"20,000 pounds-per-square-foot or greater\"; soils can \"expand up to 20% by volume when exposed to water.\" Checked 2026-09-07.",
      "url": "https://coloradogeologicalsurvey.org/hazards/expansive-soil-rock/",
      "checked": "2026-09-08"
     },
     {
      "label": "Colorado Geological Survey Open-File Report 95-05",
      "cite": "Reports Thompson's (1992b) study of 9,040 houses across 51 Denver metropolitan subdivisions: 1,056 houses built on near-surface Pierre Shale had a damage rate of 38.44 percent, against an 8.95 percent average for all houses in the study. Also: \"the granular fill material is able to distribute heave displacements from underlying expansive beds in an outwardly shifting, grain-by-grain manner, thus decreasing the magnitude and locus of any underground linear heave.\" Checked 2026-09-07.",
      "url": "https://coloradogeologicalsurvey.org/wp-content/uploads/woocommerce_uploads/OF-95-05.pdf",
      "checked": "2026-09-08"
     },
     {
      "label": "Colorado Geological Survey Special Publication 14 — Home Landscaping and Maintenance on Swelling Soil",
      "cite": "\"The use of plastic sheeting is discouraged because it tends to trap moisture and prevent normal evaporation from occurring. Non-woven geotextile fabrics control weeds and retard infiltration, but still permit evaporation.\" Recommended slope within the first 10 feet of a foundation is 10 percent (12 inches of vertical fall over 10 feet). Checked 2026-09-07.",
      "url": "https://coloradogeologicalsurvey.org/wp-content/uploads/woocommerce_uploads/SP-14.pdf",
      "checked": "2026-09-08"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "h2": "Check artificial-turf eligibility with your water provider",
    "body": [
     "Denver Water's turfgrass removal discount runs up to $750 through Resource Central, requires removing at least 200 square feet, and states that artificial turf is not allowed in the replacement landscape. Its HOA and commercial program will not fund designs that include artificial turf either.",
     "Thornton Water pays for it. Thornton Water offers up to $2 per square foot on 200 to 1,000 square foot projects, capped at $2,000 per lot, and requires a Minor Development Permit before installation, backing that drains at least two inches per hour, PFAS-free documentation, and no more than 25 percent coverage in a front yard.",
     "Denver Water has declared a Stage 1 drought: mandatory outdoor watering restricted to two assigned days a week, and only before 10 a.m. or after 6 p.m. On August 26, 2026 its board declared an end to the lawn watering season, prohibiting all spray irrigation after September 30. Aurora's Stage 1 rules state that no new cool-weather turf can be installed."
    ],
    "sources": [
     {
      "label": "Denver Water — turfgrass removal discount",
      "cite": "\"Application requirements include the removal of at least 200 square feet of water-intensive turf, a photo of the area to be transformed and plans for the new water-wise landscape that will be created (artificial turf is not allowed).\" Discount of up to $750 in 2026, administered by Resource Central. Checked 2026-09-07.",
