    "h2": "Preparation depends on the property and use",
    "body": [
     "Expansive soils are a regional planning consideration in the Denver area. The Colorado Geological Survey reports that half of the roughly thirty sedimentary bedrock formations exposed in the Front Range Urban Corridor contain swelling clay, and that those formations underlie all of the major cities. In a study of 9,040 Denver-metro houses reported in CGS Open-File Report 95-05, houses on near-surface Pierre Shale had a 38.44 percent damage rate against an 8.95 percent study average, and where alluvium lay over the same shale the rate was 9.49 percent.",
     "Coastal soil mapping raises different questions about drainage and groundwater. The USDA Official Series Description for Leon fine sand, one of the dominant series in Brunswick County, puts the water table at 6 to 18 inches for one to four months in most years, and 0 to 6 inches in low flats. In northeast Florida, SSURGO mapping makes poorly and very poorly drained soils the dominant condition on 71.4 percent of mapped Duval acres, 78.5 percent of St. Johns and 82.2 percent of Nassau.",
     "The turf itself settles neither question. New Jersey DEP's 2025 review of the science states that a product draining at ten inches per hour does not mean the ground underneath will infiltrate at that rate. Neither regional mapping nor product permeability establishes that every installation will stay flat or drain after every storm."
