    "h2": "Indoor, sports and removal ask different questions",
    "body": [
     "Outdoors, sunlight runs a clock on the fiber. A UN Environment Programme assessment reports non-stabilized HDPE embrittled after 300 hours of accelerated exposure, and little degradation out to 1,300 hours once a UV absorber and hindered amine light stabilizers are both used. New Jersey DEP's 2025 review calls shade from the built environment and from tree canopies the most promising mitigation techniques. Pfautsch and colleagues recorded 30 mm fibers at 57.5 degrees Celsius in sun and 32.2 in shade.",
     "For an indoor floor, confirm the existing surface, attachment, pad and any infill rather than importing an outdoor soil-base specification. Plan cleaning and reopening around the selected system. The Jacksonville Jaguars’ Miller Electric Center is an outside project using coconut fiber and cork infill; it does not establish a Timeless performance promise.",
     "Replacement has tests of its own. C.R.S. 37-99-103(4)(a) preserves anything installed before 1 January 2026, so nothing already in the ground has to come out. On a covered Florida lot, Rule 62-308.100 (2)(b) requires the system itself to be disposable at a landfill permitted under Chapter 62-701, F.A.C. And North Carolina's built-upon-area exclusion at G.S. 143-214.7D(b)(6) only holds if the turf drains through its backing and was installed to the manufacturer's specification over a pervious surface."
