    "h2": "Four states, four different answers",
    "body": [
     "Colorado regulates the water. SB24-005 created Article 99 of Title 37 in 2024 and banned artificial turf outright on covered property. HB25-1113 rewrote C.R.S. 37-99-103(1) in 2025 to insert the word NONFUNCTIONAL in front of \"artificial turf,\" and took effect 6 August 2025. The prohibition binds local entities from 1 January 2026 and reaches commercial, institutional and HOA-common property. Single-family homes are outside it.",
     "North Carolina regulates the runoff, and it decided in the installer's favor. G.S. 143-214.7D(b)(6) says artificial turf manufactured to drain through its backing and installed to the manufacturer's specification over a pervious surface is not built-upon area, and subsection (d) bars a local government from adopting a definition that does not comply.",
     "South Carolina we found nothing on, in the codes we searched or at state level. We found no equivalent statute, and none of the South Carolina codes we read names artificial turf at all. Horry County's Sec. 17.7-16 counts \"compacted gravel and soil surfaces, awnings and other fabric or plastic coverings\" as impervious without naming turf. North Myrtle Beach's Sec. 23-129.4, added by Ord. No. 25-45 on 6 October 2025, is the one written route through, and only on single-family and duplex work not subject to site-specific development plan review. The surface counts as pervious if it demonstrates a minimum infiltration rate of two inches per hour on manufacturer testing or accepted industry standards, is installed to manufacturer specification, passes inspection and is maintained. \"If these criteria are not met, the surface will be considered impervious, regardless of material type or labeling.\"",
     "Florida regulates the build. Section 125.572, Florida Statutes, created by HB 683 in 2025, stops a local government prohibiting compliant synthetic turf on single-family residential property once DEP adopts standards, and DEP adopted them as Rule 62-308.100, F.A.C., effective 19 May 2026. DEP's own FAQ narrows what that means: the rule applies to single-family residential property of one acre or less, DEP issues no permits, runs no inspection or enforcement program, and does not address HOA requirements."
    ],
